    "purpose": "Introduces the wooden wok spatula Kenji co-designed with Earlywood for flat-bottom woks, explaining why he made it and showing how its shape works.",
    "video_type": "review",
    "thesis": "No existing spatulas suited a 14-inch flat-bottom wok, so Kenji worked with Earlywood through many iterations to design one whose flat edge and gentle curve match that wok's shape.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "In The Wok, Kenji recommends a 14-inch carbon steel flat-bottom wok for Western stovetops and a round-bottom wok for people with a wok range.",
      "He found no spatulas designed specifically for flat-bottom woks, so he co-designed one with Earlywood, a Montana company that makes many of his tools.",
      "The design went through dozens of iterations sent back and forth before the final version.",
      "The spatula has a flat area for the wok's flat base and a gentle curve that follows the side, giving contact along the whole edge for stir-frying, flipping, tossing and scraping.",
      "It is handed: there are right-handed and left-handed models, a trade-off for the ergonomic shape.",
      "Kenji says he makes no money from the spatula, but Earlywood also sells signed copies of his books, which do support him."
    ],
